Write a Python command-line script that reports the size of a GitHub repository.
It must run on Termux (Android) under a Termux-style Python shebang, and it uses
only the standard library's regex, system-argument, and future-annotations
features plus the third-party requests package.
Behavior and requirements: 1.
Entry point: the script runs when executed directly.
It expects exactly one command-line argument (besides the script name).
If the argument count is not exactly two, print a usage message in the form
"Usage: python get-repo-size <user/repo> or <https://github.com/user/repo>" and
exit with status code 1. 2.
Input parsing: the single argument is either a full GitHub URL beginning with
"https://github.com/" or a shorthand "user/repo" string. - For a full URL,
extract the owner and repository name with a regular expression matching the
github.com domain followed by two path segments (owner and repo, each being
non-slash characters).
Extra path segments after the repo (such as /tree/main) should be ignored.
If the regex does not match, print "Invalid GitHub URL format." and return
without error. - For a shorthand, split on "/" and require exactly two parts.
If the count is not two, print "Invalid format.
Use 'user/repo' or full URL." and return.
Do not otherwise validate characters. 3.
API request: build the URL "https://api.github.com/repos/{user}/{repo}" and
perform an HTTP GET with the requests library, with no custom headers,
authentication token, or timeout. - If the response status is 404, print
"Repository '{user}/{repo}' not found." and return. - Otherwise call
raise_for_status so that other HTTP errors raise an exception. - Parse the
response body as JSON and read the "size" field (defaulting to 0 if missing).
GitHub reports this size in kilobytes. 4.
Output: convert kilobytes to megabytes by dividing by 1024.
Print two lines: - "Repository: {user}/{repo}" - "Size: {size_mb} MB" where
size_mb is formatted with exactly two decimal places. 5.
Error handling: wrap the network request and parsing in a try block catching the
requests library's base RequestException.
On such an error, print "Error fetching data: " followed by the exception
message.
Do not catch other exception types. 6.
Style: use postponed evaluation of type annotations (from __future__ import
annotations).
The main function takes a string and returns nothing, with type hints.
Use a Termux-specific shebang line pointing to the Termux Python interpreter
path.
Keep the whole program in a single file with no classes and no external
configuration.
Use only print for output, with no logging module. 7.
Usage examples the script should support: - python get-repo-size psf/requests -
python get-repo-size https://github.com/psf/requests Deliver the script as one
self-contained Python file implementing exactly this behavior. ---
